The uncompiled model and compiled model are separate objects. As much as possible, they work in the same way. (Some steps like setting inits and data do not really make sense with the compiled model, because an algorithm's setup steps will typically want to look at those, and that will be done with the uncompiled model.)

When the compiled model is created, the current values (variables and log probabilities) are copied from the uncompiled model. After that, any operations with either one are distinct from the other.

The uncompiled model will always be slower. For computationally intense purposes like MCMC, it is almost always worth compiling the model (and MCMC) first. If the simulation step is not too slow with the uncompiled model, it is completely fine to do it with the uncompiled model.

With the compiled model, say Cmodel, calling `Cmodel$simulate(nodes)` from R can still be slow because the iteration over nodes is done in R (even though each node's simulation is done in C++). To make it really fast, one needs to write a nimbleFunction that uses the model and contains the `model$simulate(nodes)` in a run function (not setup function). Then the simulation over nodes will be done in C++.

A compiled nimbleFunction (e.g. an MCMC or other algorithm) that uses a model will automatically use the compiled version of the model.

Does that answer your question?
Perry

